# Internet Culture Hub — Media Architecture Rules

These rules apply to **all** AI-generated content in `lib/content/`.  
They are enforced to keep the media system consistent and production-ready.

---

## THE ONE SYSTEM: `media?: MediaItem[]`

- **Never use** `imageUrl`, `thumbnailUrl`, or `mediaEmbeds` fields.
- **Always use** `media?: MediaItem[]` for all media content.
- No other media field will ever be added to `BaseEntry`.

---

## MediaItem role rules

Every `MediaItem` must have a `role` field. Role controls placement:

| Role | Placement |
|------|-----------|
| `"featured"` | Canonical visual: card thumbnail + article hero + Open Graph. ONE per article. |
| `"supporting"` | Image gallery section inside the article (never used as card/hero). |
| `"video"` | Video section inside the gallery. |
| `"reference"` | Link card at the bottom of the gallery. |

**Maximum one `role:"featured"` image or gif per article.**  
If you have two candidate images, pick the most representative one; set the other to `role:"supporting"`.

**Pipeline (single source of truth):**
`role:"featured"` image/gif → `getEntryPreviewImageUrl()` → cards, detail heroes, search, related, social preview.
Do not pick different hero images in page components. Supporting images never become the card/hero.

---

## Encyclopedia appropriateness (featured images)

Before accepting any `role:"featured"` image, ask:

> Would this be appropriate as the first image on a public encyclopedia-style website?

If the answer is **no**, reject it — even if it is technically related and licensed.

**Prefer:**
- Clean, canonical representations of the topic
- Official artwork, product packaging, defining stills, neutral portraits
- Images a parent or teacher would not find awkward on a listing card

**Reject as featured (even if related):**
- Sexualized, “booth babe,” or heavily midriff/cleavage-focused fashion shots
- Extreme / abrasive deep-fried or shock-edit examples used as the hero
- Graphic violence, gore, or gratuitous shock content
- Low-effort or chaotic edits when a cleaner canonical option exists

For format memes that are *defined* by distortion (e.g. deep-fried faces): use a clean explanatory still if one exists; otherwise leave the hero empty and document via `reference` / gallery — **correct empty is better than a bad hero**.

---

## Required fields on every MediaItem

Every `MediaItem` **must** include all of the following:

```typescript
{
  role: "featured" | "supporting" | "video" | "reference",
  type: "image" | "gif" | "video" | "embed",
  url: "",        // Direct, working URL to the actual media file or page
  title: "",      // Short human-readable title
  source: "",     // Name of the source (e.g. "Wikimedia Commons", "YouTube / Channel")
  sourceUrl: "",  // URL of the source PAGE (not the raw file URL)
  platform: "",   // One of: youtube | wikimedia | knowyourmeme | original | other
  attribution: "", // Who to credit
  verified: false, // MUST be false for AI-suggested items — humans confirm and set true
}
```

Optional but strongly recommended: `license`, `description`, `date`, `tags`.

---

## URL rules

### Verified sources (use these)
- **Wikimedia Commons**: `https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/HASH/HASH/Filename.jpg`
  - Use the direct full-file URL — NEVER the `/thumb/` CDN path.
- **YouTube thumbnails**: `https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VIDEO_ID/hqdefault.jpg`
  - Use `hqdefault.jpg` — NOT `maxresdefault.jpg`. `maxresdefault` is only generated for some videos and is frequently 404.
- **YouTube videos**: `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VIDEO_ID`

### Before using any YouTube video as a media source
Verify the video is publicly available via the oembed API:
```
https://www.youtube.com/oembed?url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VIDEO_ID&format=json
```
- If the response is `Not Found` → video is deleted or private → do NOT use this ID
- If the response returns a JSON object with `html` containing an `<iframe>` → video is embeddable ✅

### Forbidden sources
- Google Images, image search thumbnails
- Instagram, TikTok, Twitter/X media CDN (`pbs.twimg.com`, `cdninstagram.com`) — hotlink-blocked, short-lived
- Facebook CDN (`fbcdn.net`) — ephemeral
- Reddit CDN (`redd.it`, `preview.redd.it`) — unstable
- Wikimedia `/thumb/` CDN paths
- Unofficial reposts or watermarked screenshots
- Random image hosts with no clear licensing (Pinterest, Gyazo, `prnt.sc`, `postimg.cc`)
- `maxresdefault.jpg` YouTube thumbnails — use `hqdefault.jpg` instead

---

## `verified` field rules

- AI-suggested media: **always** set `verified: false`
- Human has opened and confirmed the URL loads the correct image: set `verified: true`
- Never set `verified: true` in AI-generated content
- The audit script (`npm run audit:media`) will warn on unverified items
- Optional live checks: `npm run audit:media:live` (HEAD + YouTube oEmbed) — **never** auto-sets `verified: true`

---

## Category-specific media rules

### Memes
- Featured image required if the meme is visually defined (Doge, Pepe, Nyan Cat)
- Gradient fallback acceptable for abstract or text-only memes
- Videos go in `role:"video"`, not as the featured item
- Know Your Meme and Wikipedia links go in `role:"reference"`

### Creators
- Featured photo strongly recommended — prefer Wikimedia Commons CC-licensed photos
- Never invent or guess creator photos — if no CC-licensed source exists, leave without media
- A reference to their main channel is helpful as `role:"reference"`

### Events
- Featured image of the defining moment (Wikimedia CC or YouTube thumbnail)
- Video item if a specific video defined the event
- Gradient fallback is acceptable if no CC-licensed image can be found

### Trends
- Most trends do NOT need media — gradient fallback is fine
- Only add media if the trend has a distinct visual identity

### Slang
- Almost never needs media
- Only add media if a specific visual directly explains the term's origin

---

## Category-aware media expectations

| Category | Media |
|---|---|
| Meme | Featured image/gif when visually defined — warn if missing |
| Creator | Featured photo when a reliable CC/Commons source exists — warn if missing |
| Event | Defining moment/object media when possible — warn if missing |
| Slang | Optional — gradient is fine; **do not** treat as a defect |
| Trend | Optional unless visually defined — gradient is fine |

## After adding an article — mandatory verification

Run:
```bash
npm run validate          # hard content + media schema gates
npm run audit:media       # offline media readiness report
npm run audit:media:live  # optional HEAD + YouTube oEmbed (warnings only)
npm run build             # includes validate via prebuild
```

`verified:true` remains human-controlled after live checks pass.

### Media verification checklist (must pass before article is complete)

**Featured image**
- [ ] Appropriate as the first image on a public encyclopedia site (see rule above)
- [ ] Clean/canonical representation — not an extreme or awkward related example
- [ ] URL returns an image — verified by opening it in a browser or with `curl -I URL`
- [ ] Uses `hqdefault.jpg` for YouTube thumbnails (never `maxresdefault.jpg`)
- [ ] Hero image loads on the article page
- [ ] Card thumbnail loads on the category listing

**Videos**
- [ ] Video ID verified via oembed before use — returns embeddable iframe
- [ ] Video plays in the gallery player
- [ ] Video is the correct content

**All items**
- [ ] No broken image icons
- [ ] No 404 URLs
- [ ] No ephemeral/suspicious hosts
- [ ] Captions display correctly

---

## AI workflow for future media suggestions

When suggesting media for an article:
1. Identify the most representative media for each role.
2. Run the encyclopedia appropriateness test on any featured candidate — reject if it fails.
3. Prefer clean canonical over extreme related examples.
4. Set `verified: false` on every item.
5. Add a comment explaining why each URL was chosen.
6. A human will verify each URL and update `verified: true`.

```typescript
// AI suggested — human must verify URL and set verified: true
{
  role: "featured",
  type: "image",
  url: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/.../Filename.jpg",
  // ... other fields ...
  verified: false,
}
```
